<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # So Many Different Historical Events to Learn! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> So Many Different Historical Events to Learn!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Concept: Explain cultural concepts or idioms to others. Text: Summarize complex texts for peers.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Can explain basic historical and cultural concepts, using visuals and simple definitions (e.g., "Independence means freedom from another country," while showing a timeline on the board). Can summarize key points from a history text by listing dates and events on the board (e.g., "1903: Separation of Panama from Colombia" and "1914: Canal Completed"). Can help others understand historical terms with gestures and pointing (e.g., "Colony" as "when another country is in control," with a map).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher projects images of famous Panamanian historical events (e.g., Separation in 1903, Canal construction, Reversion). Students quickly review key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>independence, separation, canal, construction, legacy</i>) on the board. In pairs, students are challenged to explain one image to their partner in 30 seconds, using simple language. This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons on historical events and sets the stage for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a complex term simply. For example, the teacher displays "colonial period" and says: "Imagine a country, like Panama, where another, more powerful country is in control of everything – the laws, the economy, the culture. This period is called the <i>colonial period</i>. Panama was once a <i>colony</i> of Spain, then of Colombia." The teacher emphasizes clarity and simple definitions. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /kəˈloʊ.ni.əl/ vs. /ˈkɑl.ə.ni/ and highlights the stress pattern in "research" (noun: /ˈriː.sɜːrtʃ/, verb: /rɪˈsɜːrtʃ/), and the /d/, /t/, /ɪd/ endings for past tense verbs like <i>declared, accomplished, suggested</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I explain a difficult word in simpler terms, am I mediating?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I summarize a long text for my friends, do I need to include every single detail, or just the main points?" (Expected answer: Just the main points, simplified)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio excerpt (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) from a historical podcast, where a historian discusses the <i>significance</i> of Panama's <i>separation</i> from Colombia in 1903 and its impact on the <i>construction</i> of the Canal. The excerpt will use past simple and reported speech, e.g., "Historians say the <i>separation</i> was a critical turning point..." or "The Canal <i>was completed</i> in 1914, transforming global trade <i>routes</i>." Students also receive a short, written summary of the podcast transcript with some key terms highlighted (<i>independence, transformation, economy, identity</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the main points and relevant details in spoken historical explanations and written summaries, identifying specific historical events, dates, and their reported significance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main historical event discussed in this podcast excerpt?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen/read and mark statements as True or False (e.g., "The Panama Canal was completed before Panama declared independence." "Historians agree the Canal's construction had no impact on Panama's economy.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match dates (e.g., 1903, 1914) with key events (<i>separation, Canal completed</i>) mentioned in the excerpt.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using Past simple, Reported speech, and Passive voice to explain historical events and concepts. Vocabulary: <i>history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, economy, diversity, events, culture, identity, transformation, legacy</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Concept Explanation Chain (Controlled Practice):</b> Teacher provides a list of complex historical terms (e.g., <i>revolution, legacy, heritage, engineering marvel, colonial period</i>). Students work in small groups. One student writes a simple definition for a term, then passes it to the next student who has to rephrase it even simpler or add an example. The goal is to mediate the concept step-by-step for someone with less knowledge. Teacher monitors, providing feedback on clarity and accurate use of vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>"Historians Say..." Transformation (Semi-controlled):</b> Students are given short historical statements (e.g., "The Treaty of Culebra transferred the canal to Panama in 1979." "The U.S. played a key role in the early <i>construction</i> of the canal."). In pairs, they transform these statements into reported speech using phrases like "Historians say that...", "Sources report that...", or "It <i>was reported</i> that...". They practice saying these aloud, focusing on the correct stress for 'report' (verb vs. noun) and -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Summarize and Explain (Pair Interaction):</b> Students are given short paragraphs about different historical events (e.g., the *significance* of Panama's diverse *culture*, the *impact* of the *canal* on the *economy*). Student A summarizes the paragraph in 2-3 sentences, using past simple and passive voice. Student B then asks for clarification on a specific point or concept from the summary (e.g., "Why do you believe independence shaped Panama's national identity?" or "Can you explain what '<i>transformation</i>' means in this context?"). Student A mediates by explaining further, using visuals or gestures if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Historical Event Podcast Segment Practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 3-4, continuing their work on the "Historical Event Podcast" project. Each group must prepare a 2-3 minute segment for their podcast where they:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Introduce a historical event relevant to Panama (e.g., <i>separation, canal construction, transfer of canal</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Explain any complex cultural or historical concepts related to it, simplifying them for a general audience.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Summarize the key points and impact of the event, referencing historians' views using reported speech (e.g., "Historians say that the <i>construction</i> of the canal significantly <i>transformed</i> the global *economy* and *routes*.") and passive voice (e.g., "The canal <i>was completed</i> in 1914").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. One student in the group acts as a "listener/interviewer" who may ask a clarifying question about the historical facts or concepts (e.g., "When did Panama declare <i>independence</i>?" or "What impact did the canal have on Panama's national <i>identity</i>?"). The other students must be able to mediate the information effectively.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups present their segment to another group, who acts as the audience, providing initial peer feedback on clarity and understanding. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually complete a "Podcast Mediation Reflection Sheet" based on their group's performance and the feedback received. Each student will also record a 1-minute audio snippet of their part in the podcast segment, demonstrating their ability to explain a concept or summarize historical facts using reported speech and the passive voice. This snippet can be submitted via a digital platform.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Did they simplify complex concepts effectively? (Mediation)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of summary: Were the key historical points accurately presented? (Mediation)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of reported speech: Was "Historians say..." or similar structures used correctly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of past simple and passive voice for historical events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of /s/-/z/, /ð/-/θ/, and -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>significance, transformation, identity, legacy</i>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging historical concept or event to explain clearly to your peers, and how did you simplify it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did you use reported speech to summarize a historian's view in your podcast segment? Give an example."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"After preparing your podcast segment, what did you learn about the importance of explaining historical events clearly to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific mediation strategies (e.g., simple definitions, examples, visuals, gestures) did you find most helpful in making complex historical facts understandable?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson successfully brought together all the skills towards the podcast project. The mediation activities allowed students to actively apply summarizing and explaining skills. The practice of reported speech and passive voice for historical facts was well-integrated. Some students struggled with consistently using natural intonation for reported speech. I noticed the pronunciation of "research" (noun vs. verb) needed more focused attention for certain students. For future iterations, I could provide more diverse examples of historical figures' quotes to be mediated into reported speech, and encourage more interactive debate within groups regarding historical interpretations to further engage the sociolinguistic competence. The project provided a meaningful context for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> As this is Lesson 5, the next steps will be to finalize the recording of the "Historical Event Podcast" projects, incorporating the feedback from this session, and preparing for a school-wide or class presentation/listening party for their completed mediation products.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>